WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.491
This lesson becomes cleaner as soon as the learner chooses the correct timescale.

2
00:00:05.491 --> 00:00:10.982
Day and night are daily effects, so the first motion to use is Earth's rotation, not its yearly orbit.

3
00:00:10.982 --> 00:00:16.474
Steady sunlight means one main light direction.

4
00:00:16.474 --> 00:00:21.965
Sunlight arrives from one main direction while Earth turns beneath it.

5
00:00:21.965 --> 00:00:27.456
Lit half means one side day, one side night.

6
00:00:27.456 --> 00:00:32.948
At any moment half of Earth is lit and half is in darkness.

7
00:00:32.948 --> 00:00:38.439
Daily cause means Earth rotates.

8
00:00:38.439 --> 00:00:43.930
A city moves from day to night because Earth spins, carrying the city across the boundary.

9
00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:49.421
Weak answers often jump to Earth's orbit or to distance from the Sun, even though those are the wrong timescale for sunrise and sunset.

10
00:00:49.421 --> 00:00:54.912
Once the lit-half picture stays visible, the worked example becomes a direct geometry explanation.

11
00:00:54.912 --> 00:01:00.404
Lesson 3 is strongest when day and night are tied to Earth's rotation under steady sunlight.

12
00:01:00.404 --> 00:01:05.895
Keep this takeaway in view: Use Earth's rotation first when the change happens over hours, not over a year.

13
00:01:05.895 --> 00:01:11.386
Steady sunlight means one light direction.

14
00:01:11.386 --> 00:01:16.878
The Sun does not need to move around Earth once a day to create day and night.

15
00:01:16.878 --> 00:01:22.369
Lit half means one bright side, one dark side.

16
00:01:22.369 --> 00:01:27.860
At any moment different places sit on different sides of the day-night boundary.

17
00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:36.071
This diagram matters because it shows the one feature students need most: the day-night boundary only makes sense if sunlight direction stays steady while Earth rotates.

18
00:01:36.071 --> 00:01:44.282
Light geometry matters because One half of Earth faces the Sun and one half faces away at the same moment.

19
00:01:44.282 --> 00:01:52.493
Location tracking matters because A city changes from night to day because Earth's rotation carries it across the boundary.

20
00:01:52.493 --> 00:02:00.704
Timescale clue matters because The daily change belongs to rotation, while orbit explains the much longer yearly cycle.

21
00:02:00.704 --> 00:02:08.915
Without the diagram, students often know the word rotation but never connect it to the actual light-dark geometry.

22
00:02:08.915 --> 00:02:17.126
The worked example becomes easier once the learner can point to the lit half and the turning Earth at the same time.

23
00:02:17.126 --> 00:02:25.337
Use the diagram to link Earth's spin directly to the moving day-night boundary.

24
00:02:25.337 --> 00:02:33.548
Keep this takeaway in view: The day-night diagram is strongest when light geometry and rotation stay on the same board.

25
00:02:33.548 --> 00:02:41.759
Light geometry: Keep the lit-half picture visible at every stage of the explanation.

26
00:02:41.759 --> 00:02:49.970
Location tracking: Follow one city as Earth's spin carries it into and out of daylight.

27
00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:55.371
A globe-and-lamp analogy helps because it keeps the lighting direction steady while the surface turns.

28
00:02:55.371 --> 00:03:00.772
Different places move into the light or out of it because the globe rotates, not because the lamp races around the globe once a day.

29
00:03:00.772 --> 00:03:06.174
Fixed lamp means steady light direction.

30
00:03:06.174 --> 00:03:11.575
The light does not need to loop around the globe to create changing daylight.

31
00:03:11.575 --> 00:03:16.976
Turning globe means different places rotate through the beam.

32
00:03:16.976 --> 00:03:22.378
The turning surface carries one location into the lit side and another into darkness.

33
00:03:22.378 --> 00:03:27.779
Lesson gain means daily cause becomes obvious.

34
00:03:27.779 --> 00:03:33.180
The analogy protects the learner from blaming the yearly orbit for an hours-long change.

35
00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:38.581
The analogy is useful because it corrects the most common wrong picture without needing any heavy mathematics.

36
00:03:38.581 --> 00:03:43.983
That visual habit is exactly what the worked example wants the learner to bring to the explanation.

37
00:03:43.983 --> 00:03:49.384
A fixed light and a rotating globe explain day and night more cleanly than a moving-Sun picture.

38
00:03:49.384 --> 00:03:54.785
Keep this takeaway in view: Use the globe-and-lamp picture when day and night need explaining quickly.

39
00:03:54.785 --> 00:04:00.186
Fixed lamp means steady light direction.

40
00:04:00.186 --> 00:04:05.588
The source direction stays steady while the surface moves.

41
00:04:05.588 --> 00:04:10.989
Turning globe means locations move through the beam.

42
00:04:10.989 --> 00:04:16.390
Rotation carries each place into daylight and later into darkness.

43
00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:24.231
This lesson uses short relations that act like sorting rules.

44
00:04:24.231 --> 00:04:32.072
One day belongs to one rotation of Earth, and the lit-half picture explains why different places experience daylight at different times.

45
00:04:32.072 --> 00:04:39.912
First move matters because Choose Earth's rotation first because the event is happening over hours.

46
00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:47.753
Second move matters because Keep the one-lit-half geometry visible while the location crosses the boundary.

47
00:04:47.753 --> 00:04:55.594
Third move matters because Save Earth's orbit for year-scale questions instead of forcing it into a daily one.

48
00:04:55.594 --> 00:05:03.435
Keep these relations visible: 1 day = 1 rotation of Earth; at any moment one half of Earth is lit and one half is dark; and Earth's orbit explains the year, not the daily light-dark cycle.

49
00:05:03.435 --> 00:05:11.275
Use the daily timescale first whenever the question is about sunrise, sunset, or moving from daylight into darkness over a few hours.

50
00:05:11.275 --> 00:05:19.116
A weak answer says something true about Earth orbiting the Sun but still misses the actual cause of sunrise and sunset.

51
00:05:19.116 --> 00:05:26.957
The worked example becomes much shorter once the learner has already sorted the motions by timescale.

52
00:05:26.957 --> 00:05:34.798
Use the relation board to sort daily change from yearly change before you explain anything else.

53
00:05:34.798 --> 00:05:42.638
Keep this takeaway in view: The relation board keeps daily and yearly motions from getting mixed up.

54
00:05:42.638 --> 00:05:50.479
First move: Use rotation as the first daily cause.

55
00:05:50.479 --> 00:05:58.320
Second move: Keep the lit-half picture visible while the location crosses the boundary.

56
00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:03.590
A dependable routine for Lesson 3 has three moves.

57
00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:08.860
First ask which timescale the event belongs to.

58
00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:14.130
Second draw or imagine one lit half and one dark half.

59
00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:19.400
Third track one location through the boundary using Earth's rotation.

60
00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:24.670
Step 1 means choose the daily timescale.

61
00:06:24.670 --> 00:06:29.940
Hours-long changes belong to Earth's rotation, not to its yearly orbit.

62
00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:35.210
Step 2 means keep the boundary visible.

63
00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:40.480
The lit-half picture explains why different places can have different daylight conditions at the same moment.

64
00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:45.750
Step 3 means track one location.

65
00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:51.020
Follow one city or region as Earth spins it from dark into light or from light into darkness.

66
00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:56.290
If students skip the boundary picture, they often say the word rotation but still cannot explain what the rotation is doing to the location.

67
00:06:56.290 --> 00:07:01.560
The worked example becomes much more direct when the learner already has a routine for timescale, boundary, and location tracking.

68
00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:06.830
The safest routine is daily timescale first, boundary picture second, location tracking third.

69
00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:12.100
Keep this takeaway in view: A timescale-and-boundary routine keeps the day-night explanation clean.

70
00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:17.370
Step 1 means daily timescale.

71
00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:22.640
Use Earth's rotation first for changes that happen over hours.

72
00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:27.910
Step 2 means boundary picture.

73
00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:33.180
Keep one lit half and one dark half visible.

74
00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:38.621
The day-night lesson becomes more concrete when the learner chooses one city and watches what the rotating Earth does to it.

75
00:07:38.621 --> 00:07:44.061
The city does not create day or night by itself.

76
00:07:44.061 --> 00:07:49.502
Earth's spin carries it across the light-dark boundary.

77
00:07:49.502 --> 00:07:54.942
Start point means one city in darkness.

78
00:07:54.942 --> 00:08:00.383
Begin by locating the city on the dark side of Earth.

79
00:08:00.383 --> 00:08:05.824
Rotation effect means city crosses the boundary.

80
00:08:05.824 --> 00:08:11.264
As Earth rotates, the city moves into the lit side and morning begins.

81
00:08:11.264 --> 00:08:16.705
Lesson gain means local story from global geometry.

82
00:08:16.705 --> 00:08:22.145
Tracking one place turns the big Earth picture into a readable daily event.

83
00:08:22.145 --> 00:08:27.586
A learner who only says 'Earth rotates' without tracking a place often sounds correct without actually explaining anything.

84
00:08:27.586 --> 00:08:33.026
The worked example becomes stronger when the answer follows the city into sunlight instead of stopping at the vocabulary word rotation.

85
00:08:33.026 --> 00:08:38.467
Track one place through the boundary to make the daily cause visible.

86
00:08:38.467 --> 00:08:43.908
Keep this takeaway in view: Following one city makes the day-night mechanism easier to see and explain.

87
00:08:43.908 --> 00:08:49.348
Start point means one city in darkness.

88
00:08:49.348 --> 00:08:54.789
Pick one location rather than describing the whole globe vaguely.

89
00:08:54.789 --> 00:09:00.229
Rotation effect means cross the boundary.

90
00:09:00.229 --> 00:09:05.670
Earth's spin carries the location into or out of daylight.

91
00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:10.713
Students often describe what they see rather than what causes it.

92
00:09:10.713 --> 00:09:15.755
The Sun does appear to move across the sky, but the stronger school-level explanation is that Earth rotates and makes that motion appear.

93
00:09:15.755 --> 00:09:20.798
What we see means apparent Sun motion.

94
00:09:20.798 --> 00:09:25.840
The Sun seems to travel from east to west across the sky during the day.

95
00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:30.882
What causes it means Earth rotates.

96
00:09:30.882 --> 00:09:35.925
Earth's spin changes our viewpoint and creates the apparent daily motion.

97
00:09:35.925 --> 00:09:40.968
Lesson gain means appearance versus cause.

98
00:09:40.968 --> 00:09:46.010
The answer becomes more rigorous when the apparent motion is separated from its physical cause.

99
00:09:46.010 --> 00:09:51.053
A weak answer treats the Sun's visible movement as if it were automatically the real cause of the day-night cycle.

100
00:09:51.053 --> 00:09:56.095
Keeping appearance and cause separate makes the worked example much cleaner.

101
00:09:56.095 --> 00:10:01.138
The Sun's daily motion is apparent, while Earth's rotation is the stronger physical cause.

102
00:10:01.138 --> 00:10:06.180
Keep this takeaway in view: Separate what appears to move from what physically causes the change.

103
00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:11.223
What we see means apparent Sun motion.

104
00:10:11.223 --> 00:10:16.265
The Sun seems to cross the sky during the day.

105
00:10:16.265 --> 00:10:21.308
What causes it means Earth rotates.

106
00:10:21.308 --> 00:10:26.350
Earth's spin creates that apparent motion from our viewpoint.

107
00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:31.689
Earth does orbit the Sun, but that true statement does not make it the right first explanation for a daily change.

108
00:10:31.689 --> 00:10:37.028
Sunrise and sunset happen over hours, while one full orbit takes a year.

109
00:10:37.028 --> 00:10:42.366
Daily event means hours.

110
00:10:42.366 --> 00:10:47.705
Sunrise, sunset, and moving from light into darkness happen on a short timescale.

111
00:10:47.705 --> 00:10:53.044
Yearly event means one orbit.

112
00:10:53.044 --> 00:10:58.383
Earth's orbital journey belongs to the much longer yearly cycle.

113
00:10:58.383 --> 00:11:03.721
Lesson gain means choose the right motion.

114
00:11:03.721 --> 00:11:09.060
Sorting the motions by timescale protects the explanation from technically true but causally weak statements.

115
00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:14.399
A weak answer often sounds scientific because it mentions Earth's orbit, but it is still the wrong first cause for the daily cycle.

116
00:11:14.399 --> 00:11:19.738
That is why the worked example rewards the learner who chooses the correct timescale before writing anything else.

117
00:11:19.738 --> 00:11:25.076
Use Earth's rotation first for the daily cycle and save orbit for the yearly one.

118
00:11:25.076 --> 00:11:30.415
Keep this takeaway in view: Correct timescale choice is the key to a strong day-night explanation.

119
00:11:30.415 --> 00:11:35.754
Daily event means hours.

120
00:11:35.754 --> 00:11:41.093
Day-night changes happen on a short timescale.

121
00:11:41.093 --> 00:11:46.431
Yearly event means one orbit.

122
00:11:46.431 --> 00:11:51.770
Earth's orbit explains the year, not the daily cycle.

123
00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:54.886
Ask four checks.

124
00:11:54.886 --> 00:11:58.002
Does one day belong to one rotation of Earth?

125
00:11:58.002 --> 00:12:01.118
Yes.

126
00:12:01.118 --> 00:12:04.234
Is one half of Earth lit at any moment?

127
00:12:04.234 --> 00:12:07.350
Yes.

128
00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:10.465
Does Earth's orbit explain the year rather than the daily light-dark cycle?

129
00:12:10.465 --> 00:12:13.581
Yes.

130
00:12:13.581 --> 00:12:16.697
Can a city move into daylight because Earth rotates?

131
00:12:16.697 --> 00:12:19.813
Yes.

132
00:12:19.813 --> 00:12:22.929
Timescale check means rotation for a day.

133
00:12:22.929 --> 00:12:26.045
The correct daily motion is already chosen.

134
00:12:26.045 --> 00:12:29.161
Geometry check means one lit half.

135
00:12:29.161 --> 00:12:32.277
The light-dark boundary is still visible.

136
00:12:32.277 --> 00:12:35.393
Tracking check means city across boundary.

137
00:12:35.393 --> 00:12:38.509
One location can now be followed through the daily change.

138
00:12:38.509 --> 00:12:41.625
Those checks show that the learner is explaining the mechanism, not just saying the word rotation.

139
00:12:41.625 --> 00:12:44.740
The quick check confirms that the day-night lesson is now tied to the correct motion and geometry.

140
00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:47.856
Keep this takeaway in view: You are ready when you can join timescale, lit-half geometry, and location tracking.

141
00:12:47.856 --> 00:12:50.972
Timescale check means rotation for a day.

142
00:12:50.972 --> 00:12:54.088
Use rotation first for daily change.

143
00:12:54.088 --> 00:12:57.204
Geometry check means one lit half.

144
00:12:57.204 --> 00:13:00.320
Keep the lit-half boundary visible.

145
00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:06.851
The worked example that follows asks why a city moves from darkness into sunlight and why orbit is the wrong first explanation.

146
00:13:06.851 --> 00:13:13.381
Start with the daily timescale.

147
00:13:13.381 --> 00:13:19.912
Then keep the lit-half boundary visible.

148
00:13:19.912 --> 00:13:26.443
Finally track the city's movement through that boundary as Earth rotates.

149
00:13:26.443 --> 00:13:32.974
First move matters because Choose Earth's rotation because the event happens over hours.

150
00:13:32.974 --> 00:13:39.504
Second move matters because Keep the lit-half picture visible instead of talking vaguely about sunrise.

151
00:13:39.504 --> 00:13:46.035
Final move matters because Track the city across the boundary as Earth spins.

152
00:13:46.035 --> 00:13:52.566
Keep these relations visible: choose Earth's rotation because the change is daily; keep one lit half and one dark half visible; and track the location across the boundary as Earth spins.

153
00:13:52.566 --> 00:13:59.096
A strong answer uses the correct timescale and the boundary geometry together.

154
00:13:59.096 --> 00:14:05.627
Once that route is secure, the example feels like clear physical reasoning rather than a memorized slogan.

155
00:14:05.627 --> 00:14:12.158
Start with the daily timescale, then use the boundary picture and location tracking together.

156
00:14:12.158 --> 00:14:18.689
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is easiest when the daily timescale and the lit-half geometry stay together.

157
00:14:18.689 --> 00:14:25.219
First move: Use rotation first because the change is daily.

158
00:14:25.219 --> 00:14:31.750
Second move: Keep the day-night boundary visible.
